# Models

- Thin layer: associations, validations, scopes, small predicates. Orchestration → use cases / jobs (`use_cases.mdc`, `ApplicationJob`). Callbacks limited to `before_validation` / `after_initialize` for normalize / defaults; avoid business workflows on `save` unless team explicitly allows.
- Simple filters → `scope`; cross-cutting SQL → query object (`query_objects.mdc`, `app/domains/**/queries/`). Tests: Shoulda-style for boilerplate; custom examples for tricky domain.
- `belongs_to` required by default; `optional: true` only when domain says optional.
- `validates :uniqueness` ↔ unique DB index always.
- Integer-backed `enum`; string enums only with documented reason.
- Concerns: ≥2 models share behavior — not one-off indirection.
- No `attr_accessor` for persistent-ish fields; virtual columns → `attribute :name, :type`.
- Normalization → `normalizes :field, with: ->(v) { … }` (Rails 7+).
- Counter cache when list UIs count associations often.
- `has_many` / `has_one`: explicit `dependent:` (`:destroy`, `:nullify`, `:restrict_with_error`, …).
- Model `presence` / required fields ↔ migration `null: false` / defaults / backfill plan (`migrations.mdc`).

```ruby
has_many :items, dependent: :destroy
enum :status, { draft: 0, published: 1 }
```
